- op: discuss  # ff43
  at: '2026-09-18T08:42:20Z'  # ff43
  by: agent  # ff43
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ff43
  clock: 7  # ff43
  nonce: ff43  # ff43
  message: "A CONCRETE INSTANCE OF THIS ROW'S COLLISION COMPLAINT, found 2026-09-18 in the\nRust SCIP backend, and it is sharper than the cross-VERSION case the row was\nfiled on.\n\nThe 2026-07-29 owner decision on this row identified the real harm as COLLISION\nrather than missing version: stable_id, shape_id and repo_fingerprint all emit a\nbare `sha256:` prefix, so three of the four schemes are mutually\nindistinguishable in an emitted value.\n\nThere is a fourth, undeclared collision INSIDE one of those fields. The\n`stable_id` field is produced by TWO DIFFERENT ALGORITHMS depending on which\nanalyzer emitted the node:\n\n  tree-sitter (rust.py, and the whole fleet via the shared factories)\n      sha256 over structured inputs -- e.g. make_variable_stable_id hashes\n      \"variable:{language}:{path}:{name}\"; the typed tier folds in\n      containing_stable_id and a file anchor (ADR-0035 v6/v7).\n\n  SCIP (scip/index.py:251-252)\n      sha256(raw SCIP moniker). The shared factories are not used at all.\n      The comment asserts \"the SCIP moniker is a globally-stable identity\" --\n      which is FALSE for `local <id>` monikers, which are document-scoped.\n\nBoth emit `sha256:<16hex>`. A consumer holding a bare stable_id cannot tell which\nalgorithm produced it, cannot tell whether two ids are comparable, and -- this is\nthe operative harm -- CANNOT TELL WHY TWO NODES THAT ARE THE SAME FUNCTION HAVE\nDIFFERENT IDS. Measured: of 52 (path, name) pairs emitted by both Rust arms on\naardvark-dns, ZERO share a stable_id.\n\nSo this row's expectation -- \"either the value embeds enough to recover its\nscheme/version, or the declarations are unambiguously the single binding for\nevery value of that kind\" -- is violated in a second way the filing did not\nanticipate: the declaration `stable_id_scheme=hypergumbo-stableid-v8` is NOT the\nsingle binding for every stable_id value, because the SCIP arm does not\nparticipate in that scheme at all and never announces it.\n\nBEARING ON THE ACCEPTED SPEC. If the fix is a per-scheme prefix (the `hgfp2:`\nprecedent the owner decision cites as the in-tree pattern), then the SCIP arm\nneeds either to adopt the shared factories or to declare its own scheme and\nprefix. A prefix scheme that assumes one algorithm per field would paper over\nthis rather than expose it.\n\nCross-ref: the parity row filed 2026-09-18 (0 of 52), and WI-jikok (the SCIP\nmoniker hash collapses 498 of 1255 Symbols). Both trace to the same decision to\nhash a raw moniker outside the identity factories.\n\nNOT ASKING for a scope change here -- this row's four-declaration spec stands. It\nis recorded so the fix is designed knowing there are two stable_id producers, not\none."  # ff43
- op: discuss  # 06c7
  at: '2026-09-18T09:58:54Z'  # 06c7
  by: agent  # 06c7
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 06c7
  clock: 8  # 06c7
  nonce: 06c7  # 06c7
  message: "2026-09-18: two data points for this row. (1) INV-kutid filed — the SCIP\nscheme's sha256(moniker) collides across crate TARGETS (`crate/`, `main().`\nin lib, bin and build.rs; 5 of 756 on aardvark-dns), i.e. the second\nundeclared scheme under the `sha256:` prefix has its own scope defect. (2)\nPR #1044 (INV-dolud) documents that the SCIP scheme is NEVER replaced by\nthe rust.py scheme in production (0 of 52), so every SCIP Symbol in a\nsurvey carries the undeclared scheme, not just the failures. Any fix for\nkutid that folds the document path into the hash should declare the\nscheme it produces, per this row."  # 06c7
